    "body": "### Problem\r\n\r\n  Users are experiencing inconsistent full-text search indices due to:\r\n  1. A critical bug in Tantivy where rollback on commit rollback to the initial moment of index was opened (https://github.com/quickwit-oss/tantivy/issues/2666)\r\n  2. Operations between commits being lost if the process crashes or is interrupted\r\n\r\n### Solution\r\n\r\n  This PR implements a consistency detection mechanism using operation stamps (opstamps) to track and recover from inconsistent index states.\r\n\r\n### How It Works\r\n\r\n  1. Opstamp Tracking: The fulltext queue now stores the final opstamp of each indexing operation instead of immediately removing objects from the queue.\r\n  2. Consistency Check on Startup:\r\n    - Read the actual opstamp from Tantivy's meta.json (the source of truth for committed operations) via CommitOpstamp() in tantivy-go\r\n    - Query the database for all objects indexed after this opstamp\r\n    - Re-index these objects since their operations were not persisted to disk\r\n    - In case no inconsistencies were found it GC all the ids with opstamp > 0 from the ft queue\r\n  3. Batch Operations: Leverages the new BatchAddAndDeleteDocumentsWithOpstamp API from tantivy-go to:\r\n    - Combine add/delete operations in a single commit for better performance\r\n    - Return the committed opstamp for tracking\r\n\r\n### Key Changes\r\n\r\n  - Modified fulltext queue to store opstamps for consistency tracking\r\n  - Added startup consistency check comparing database and index states\r\n  - Implemented re-indexing of uncommitted operations\r\n  - Added garbage collection for orphaned segment files via GCExtraFiles()\r\n  - Updated to latest tantivy-go which disables rollback to avoid the corruption bug\r\n  - Added comprehensive tests for consistency scenarios",
